From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 11:26:04 +0200
Subject: net: dsa: lantiq: hold the xRX500 switch macro's clocks

Neither switch node named its clocks and the driver claimed none, so the
only thing keeping the switch running was that no consumer existed to
account for the gate.

Name both clocks on both nodes and take them at probe with the managed bulk
helper, which also lets them go when the device is unbound. Both are
already running when the driver binds, so nothing changes at run time; what
the tree gains is a statement of what the block needs, and the framework
gains a consumer to count.

The two macros take different pairs because they sit on different branches
of the same PLL: the switch divider and gate for one, the packet
accelerator's for the other.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
index 34262d6ac90f..7a86b6047528 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
@@ -274,10 +274,17 @@ ebu_nand: nand-controller@16f00000 {
 	 * dma-noncoherent is not decoration. The PHY cores fetch their
 	 * firmware without taking part in the platform's cache coherence, so
 	 * the staged image has to be written back before they are released.
+	 *
+	 * Each macro is fed by a divider off the second PLL and a gate of its
+	 * own, and neither is shared with anything else on the die. The two
+	 * macros sit on different branches of that PLL, which is why the pair
+	 * differs between them.
 	 */
 	gswl: switch@1c000000 {
 		compatible = "lantiq,xrx500-gswip";
 		reg = <0x1c000000 0x4000>;
+		clocks = <&cgu CLK_GSWIP>, <&cgu GCLK_GSWIP>;
+		clock-names = "freq", "gate";
 		dsa,member = <0 0>;
 		dma-noncoherent;
 		status = "disabled";
@@ -307,6 +314,8 @@ fixed-link {
 	gswr: switch@1a000000 {
 		compatible = "lantiq,xrx500-gswip-r";
 		reg = <0x1a000000 0x4000>;
+		clocks = <&cgu CLK_PAE>, <&cgu GCLK_PAE>;
+		clock-names = "freq", "gate";
 		dsa,member = <1 0>;
 		dma-noncoherent;
 		status = "disabled";
diff --git a/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c b/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c
index bf5ae005e0cf..db94567a0862 100644
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_xrx500.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include "lantiq_gswip.h"
 #include "lantiq_pce_xrx500.h"
 
+#include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/firmware.h>
@@ -1081,6 +1082,7 @@ static const struct regmap_config gswip_xrx500_regmap_config = {
 static int gswip_xrx500_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct clk_bulk_data *clks;
 	struct gswip_xrx500 *chip;
 	struct gswip_priv *priv;
 	void __iomem *base;
@@ -1109,6 +1111,15 @@ static int gswip_xrx500_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, -EINVAL,
 				     "the switch node needs dma-noncoherent\n");
 
+	/* The macro's divider and its gate. Both are already running when
+	 * the driver binds, so claiming them changes nothing at run time;
+	 * what it adds is that the block needing them is the one holding
+	 * them, and that they are let go again when it is not.
+	 */
+	err = devm_clk_bulk_get_all_enabled(dev, &clks);
+	if (err < 0)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, err, "cannot enable the clocks\n");
+
 	base = devm_platform_ioremap_resource(pdev, 0);
 	if (IS_ERR(base))
 		return PTR_ERR(base);
